MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Daniel and I just stepped through an example, and BFGS
is definitely broken in the current 2.0 develop branch.

1.  There's no output.  (At least that we can reproduce
     given the documented arguments.)

2.  The termination message needs to be changed to something
     human readable.  (It doesn't match the return code of the
     program).

Also,

3.  There are no unit tests or functional tests I can find.
     If there are some, please let me know.

So I want to label this feature "experimental".  We don't have
any way to separate that or warn users.

- Bob

On 10/7/13 12:00 PM, Marcus Brubaker wrote:

> On Sun, Oct 6, 2013 at 9:12 PM, Bob Carpenter <ca...@alias-i.com <mailto:ca...@alias-i.com>> wrote:
>
>     I'm stuck on doc for optimization because I can't make
>     it work reliably (I had no problem with Stan 1.3's).
>
>     Things I found confusing:
>
>     1.  I didn't get any output from the default.  The only
>     way I get output is if I set "save_iterations=1".  But I
>     somehow don't think this is right because I don't get any
>     output with "algorithm=newton" at all, even with save_iterations=1.
>     And in other configs of BFGS, I didn't get reasonable output, making
>     me think it was an intermediate iteration.
>
>
> I believe the idea for the optimization algorithms was that it would only print a single point at the end unless
> save_iterations was specified but it's possible that this isn't happening.  I don't really test the save_iterations=0
> path much at all in my typical usage because, well, convergence almost never happens.  I just don't have that kind of
> patience :)
>
>
>     2.  When I ran, it said it was using BFGS, and
>
>          ------------------------------
>          /temp2/norm$ ./norm-fit optimize save_iterations=1
>          ...
>          initial log joint probability = -9.80884
>              Iter      log prob        ||dx||      ||grad||       alpha      alpha0  # evals  Notes
>                 1     -0.525525       1.36259   9.31988e-11         0.1           1        3
>          Optimization terminated with code 2

>          ------------------------------__------

>
>          a.  What is code 2?   And whatever it is, we should make these
>          return results a little more user friendly --- I thought I just
>          had it misconfigured until I set save_iterations=1
>
>          b.  What is code 1?  I got that in another situation when I had an
>          init file.
>
>
>
> Positive exit codes mean some kind of "normal" termination criteria.  In the above case, the norm of the gradient is
> small.  Exit code of zero means it hit the iteration limit.  Negative exit codes indicate termination for unusual
> reasons, typically indicating a badly behaved objective function.
>
>          c.  What is that debug print?  It looks BFGS specific
>
>
> It is.
>
>            i.  Is ||grad|| just the L2 norm of the gradient?
>
>
> Yes.
>
>           ii.  What is ||dx||?  Is that some kind of stepsize?
>
>
> This is the size of the step that was taken at that iteration.  I.E., if x_i is the parameters at iteration i, dx = ||
> x_i - x_{i-1} ||.
>
>          iii.  What are alpha and alpha0?
>
>
> alpha is the step size taken by the line search.  alpha0 is the first value that's guessed based on heuristics.
>   Typically, with a well-behaved objective alpha = alpha0 = 1.
>
>          We can just make the output more verbose to make these more clear.
>
>
>
> alpha0 could probably be removed.  It was put there while I was debugging some parts of the line search.  Now that
> things seem to be relatively stable, it doesn't provide much extra debugging information.
>
>
>
>     3.  Running with Newton has other problems.
>
>          ----------------------
>          ./norm-fit optimize algorithm=newton save_iterations=1
>          initial log joint probability = 0
>           Iteration  1. Log joint probability =  -0.525525. Improved by -0.525525.
>          -------------------------
>
>          a.  nothing written into output csv file other than config
>
>          b.  no error message
>
>
>     4.  Do the empty paths in the output for files mean they're null?
>          If so, some better print might help, and I can also add doc.
>
>
>
> No idea on either of these, the Newton method hasn't seen much attention.  Personally, I'd like to see it removed until
> we can get autodiff'd Hessians.
>
> Cheers,
> Marcus
>

